    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on some walnut cabinet joinery and I'm running into a frustrating issue. Whenever I'm cutting the dovetails, the wood fibers are splintering right at the tips of the pins. It's ruining the edges and I can't get a clean finish. I've checked my layout, but the splintering is persistent. Any advice on tool sharpness or maybe how I should be managing the grain direction to prevent this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It sounds like you may need to sharpen your chisels and marking gauges to ensure clean, controlled cuts. Ensure that your chisels are razor sharp. Additionally, pay close attention to grain direction—always cut with the grain to minimize splintering. Planing the tips can also help smooth out the splintered areas before finishing. Good luck with your cabinet joinery!",
      "source": "target",
