    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I've already sharpened the chisels to a mirror polish, so I don't think it's a blunt edge issue. The splintering happens even when I'm being really careful with the paring. Could it be related to the moisture content of the walnut, or is there a specific angle for the bevel that works better for preventing those micro-tears at the tips of the pins?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A critical consideration is the proper moisture level of your walnut. Too much moisture can cause wood to become too soft and easily splinter when being worked into a tight radius. Make sure the wood is adequately seasoned before starting the joinery to increase its stability. Additionally, adjusting the angle of the bevel on your chisels could indeed influence the splintering as it affects the escape path of the chips. An internal bevel angle of about 25 to 30 degrees often works well to minimize tear-out. Lastly, always use sharp sharpening stones for your tools and maintain consistent cuts to ensure cleaner splits.",
      "source": "target",
