    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The moisture levels seem fine, they've been in my shop for a few months now. I've also tried changing my paring direction, but it's like the wood is fighting me specifically at those thin pin tips. It feels almost unpredictable, like the fiber structure is just inconsistent right when I hit the very end of the cut. Is there any other way to brace the wood or maybe a specific way to approach the final paring to keep those fibers from lifting?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Considering the unpredictability you're facing, you could try using a drop form or a piece of scrap as a brace when making your final paring. This helps to provide additional support and control to the wood fibers during the cutting process, reducing the chance of splintering. Additionally, make sure that the block or brace is securely clamped and that it's wide enough to facilitate stable carving. Another technique to consider for the final paring is to work evenly around the corner by using a sharp chisel at an angle to the direction of the grain; starting at the edge and working towards the center can help maintain evenness in fiber tear-out. Remember to take frequent breaks to assess and adjust your technique incrementally, focusing on preventing tear-out rather than trying to eliminate it once it's already occurring.",
      "source": "target",
